1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:17,520
A biblical theology of generosity does not start with tithing, or even Jesus' command

2
00:00:17,520 --> 00:00:19,000
to be generous to the poor.

3
00:00:21,900 --> 00:00:27,840
It starts before time and space, with who God is.

4
00:00:27,840 --> 00:00:35,720
Since the beginning of Scripture, the world itself was created as an act of generous love.

5
00:00:35,720 --> 00:00:48,220
With God as a joyful, generous host, the world as His home, and you and I as His special guests.

6
00:00:51,900 --> 00:00:58,860
And from the inner nature of God, giving becomes our nature.

7
00:00:58,860 --> 00:01:07,400
And little by little, He makes our souls incandescent.

8
00:01:21,900 --> 00:01:31,820
Well, good morning, church.

9
00:01:31,820 --> 00:01:34,860
So good to be with you all this morning.

10
00:01:34,860 --> 00:01:39,160
If we haven't had the privilege of meeting, which unfortunately I feel like it's more and more of us,

11
00:01:39,160 --> 00:01:40,320
my name is Ryan.

12
00:01:40,320 --> 00:01:45,680
I had the privilege of being one of the pastors here, and I would love to meet you if I have not yet done so.

13
00:01:46,140 --> 00:01:53,160
If you're new or newer, we are in week four of a church-based curriculum, church-wide curriculum,

14
00:01:53,160 --> 00:01:54,320
called Practicing the Way.

15
00:01:54,320 --> 00:01:55,660
This is actually our third practice.

16
00:01:55,660 --> 00:01:57,460
At the beginning of the year, we did prayer and fasting.

17
00:01:57,460 --> 00:01:59,060
This year, we're doing generosity.

18
00:01:59,060 --> 00:02:04,040
We'll do another three next year, and then another three the year after that.

19
00:02:04,040 --> 00:02:06,640
And so we are finishing up generosity today.

20
00:02:06,640 --> 00:02:11,560
The whole goal of the curriculum is aimed at helping us apply the lifestyle and teachings of Jesus

21
00:02:11,560 --> 00:02:13,900
into our daily lives.

22
00:02:13,900 --> 00:02:17,020
And I'm super thankful for all of you that have participated,

23
00:02:17,020 --> 00:02:19,360
especially those of you that are participating in a small group.

24
00:02:19,360 --> 00:02:22,940
It just adds so much more depth to the conversation.

25
00:02:22,940 --> 00:02:28,400
You can have nuanced conversations with people that you trust about how to work these things out.

26
00:02:28,400 --> 00:02:30,740
And so I'm excited about that.

27
00:02:30,740 --> 00:02:35,400
Christ's teachings, I think you've probably noticed this last couple weeks on generosity,

28
00:02:35,400 --> 00:02:39,200
they're countercultural, and they're often difficult to know how to contextualize,

29
00:02:39,200 --> 00:02:43,560
how we should apply them specifically in our lives,

30
00:02:43,560 --> 00:02:47,160
especially in the midst of a complex global economy

31
00:02:47,160 --> 00:02:52,200
that often makes this idea of generosity even more challenging

32
00:02:52,200 --> 00:02:54,140
than some of the other teachings of Jesus.

33
00:02:54,140 --> 00:02:57,440
And so I personally just want to say to each one of you

34
00:02:57,440 --> 00:03:00,720
how thankful I am to lead a church that is willing to take the example

35
00:03:00,720 --> 00:03:04,360
and teachings of Jesus seriously, even when they're difficult,

36
00:03:04,360 --> 00:03:06,320
and to put them into practice.

37
00:03:06,320 --> 00:03:08,120
Just I was having a conversation with someone last night,

38
00:03:08,120 --> 00:03:12,560
and they said, man, give me Romans, Galatians, Philippians,

39
00:03:12,560 --> 00:03:14,180
give me all those books, and I got it.

40
00:03:14,180 --> 00:03:15,880
You make me open up to the book of Luke,

41
00:03:15,880 --> 00:03:18,080
and all of a sudden I'm like, what?

42
00:03:18,080 --> 00:03:19,640
I have to do what?

43
00:03:19,640 --> 00:03:20,360
We have to do what?

44
00:03:20,360 --> 00:03:20,840
How is this?

45
00:03:20,840 --> 00:03:22,380
You know, it just gets complicated.

46
00:03:22,380 --> 00:03:25,480
The teachings of Jesus are challenging and complicated

47
00:03:25,480 --> 00:03:26,880
and often feel overwhelming.

48
00:03:26,880 --> 00:03:30,500
And oftentimes the American church has tried our best.

49
00:03:30,500 --> 00:03:33,460
We've tried our best to explain away some of the hard teachings of Jesus.

50
00:03:34,140 --> 00:03:38,200
We've tried to use some of the New Testament to reinterpret some of Jesus' words

51
00:03:38,200 --> 00:03:41,960
so that we don't actually have to do what Jesus tells us to do.

52
00:03:41,960 --> 00:03:45,720
And I'm just discontent with that approach.

53
00:03:45,720 --> 00:03:48,760
So let's turn to Luke.

54
00:03:48,760 --> 00:03:49,460
Why don't we?

55
00:03:49,460 --> 00:03:51,160
It should be fun, right?

56
00:03:51,160 --> 00:03:55,040
Luke chapter 12 is where we're going to end up here in just a few minutes.

57
00:03:55,040 --> 00:03:58,440
This might be the longest introduction to any sermon that I've ever preached.

58
00:03:59,020 --> 00:04:01,980
So maybe 20 minutes from now, we'll get to Luke chapter 12.

59
00:04:01,980 --> 00:04:02,520
Let's pray.

60
00:04:02,520 --> 00:04:07,720
Father in heaven, we are so grateful that you sent your son Jesus

61
00:04:07,720 --> 00:04:16,440
to live a life demonstrating to us what true humanity looks like.

62
00:04:18,420 --> 00:04:22,580
How life is supposed to be lived on earth as it is in heaven.

63
00:04:22,580 --> 00:04:24,800
He lived a perfect life.

64
00:04:24,800 --> 00:04:29,540
He demonstrated grace and sacrificial love.

65
00:04:29,540 --> 00:04:30,840
And yet we crucified him.

66
00:04:30,840 --> 00:04:36,140
And it was through his sacrificial death and resurrection

67
00:04:36,140 --> 00:04:38,220
that he proved he was who he said he was.

68
00:04:38,220 --> 00:04:41,820
And he has power in his teaching and in his life.

69
00:04:42,460 --> 00:04:45,380
And therefore, everything he said is worthy to be trusted.

70
00:04:45,380 --> 00:04:48,980
Everything he said will come true, will come to pass.

71
00:04:48,980 --> 00:04:53,360
God, we have hope for the future because of the resurrection.

72
00:04:53,360 --> 00:04:55,340
Remind us of that this morning.

73
00:04:55,340 --> 00:04:59,500
Help us to have open hands with all of the possessions that you've given us

74
00:04:59,500 --> 00:05:02,580
so that we can glorify you and serve our neighbor.

75
00:05:02,580 --> 00:05:04,300
And it's in Jesus' name I pray.

76
00:05:04,300 --> 00:05:04,920
Amen.

77
00:05:04,920 --> 00:05:06,740
Do me a favor.

78
00:05:06,740 --> 00:05:10,240
If you're in here and you would consider yourself an American football fan,

79
00:05:10,240 --> 00:05:10,940
raise your hand.

80
00:05:12,060 --> 00:05:13,080
Okay, good number of us.

81
00:05:13,080 --> 00:05:15,320
Donovan made me say American football

82
00:05:15,320 --> 00:05:18,500
because he said that it's actually not the real football.

83
00:05:18,500 --> 00:05:20,580
Okay, so even if you're not a football fan,

84
00:05:20,580 --> 00:05:23,240
raise your hand if you're going to watch the Super Bowl this year probably.

85
00:05:23,240 --> 00:05:26,980
NFL Super Bowl, yeah, okay, cool.

86
00:05:26,980 --> 00:05:28,280
Yep, all right, most of us.

87
00:05:28,280 --> 00:05:30,300
And even if you're not even going to do that,

88
00:05:30,300 --> 00:05:32,220
you've probably lived in America long enough

89
00:05:32,220 --> 00:05:34,080
that you've been exposed to the rules, correct?

90
00:05:34,080 --> 00:05:35,500
Most of us know the rules of football.

91
00:05:35,500 --> 00:05:39,160
All right, well, just in case you don't know the rules,

92
00:05:39,160 --> 00:05:41,560
I'm going to do a real high-level summary

93
00:05:41,560 --> 00:05:44,040
because it's important that we're all on the same page for this.

94
00:05:44,040 --> 00:05:45,100
We've got offense.

95
00:05:45,100 --> 00:05:47,740
Offense tries to score points, right?

96
00:05:47,740 --> 00:05:49,340
We've got defense.

97
00:05:49,340 --> 00:05:50,680
Defense tries to stop,

98
00:05:50,680 --> 00:05:52,320
and all of my football fans are like,

99
00:05:52,320 --> 00:05:54,080
seriously, I have to sit through a one-on-one?

100
00:05:54,080 --> 00:05:55,120
Don't worry.

101
00:05:55,120 --> 00:05:58,560
All right, defense is trying to stop the team from scoring points.

102
00:05:58,560 --> 00:06:00,720
We've got two levels of,

103
00:06:00,720 --> 00:06:02,220
there's more than this,

104
00:06:02,220 --> 00:06:03,440
but again, I said base level.

105
00:06:03,440 --> 00:06:05,140
Two levels of scoring, right?

106
00:06:05,180 --> 00:06:05,780
There's a touchdown,

107
00:06:05,780 --> 00:06:06,980
which is worth six points,

108
00:06:06,980 --> 00:06:07,820
and there's a field goal,

109
00:06:07,820 --> 00:06:10,820
which is worth three points.

110
00:06:10,820 --> 00:06:13,220
Now, imagine with me for just a second

111
00:06:13,220 --> 00:06:16,180
that the week before the Super Bowl this year,

112
00:06:16,180 --> 00:06:18,660
the commissioner comes out,

113
00:06:18,660 --> 00:06:19,620
and he makes an announcement

114
00:06:19,620 --> 00:06:23,180
that he has uncovered special historical documents

115
00:06:23,180 --> 00:06:26,520
from the founding of the football league,

116
00:06:26,520 --> 00:06:28,040
the American Football League,

117
00:06:28,140 --> 00:06:30,080
and it turns out that we've been keeping score

118
00:06:30,080 --> 00:06:31,960
the wrong way the entire time.

119
00:06:31,960 --> 00:06:35,700
Apparently, the founders of the National Football League

120
00:06:35,700 --> 00:06:36,780
or American Football,

121
00:06:36,780 --> 00:06:39,660
they actually wanted to prioritize kicking field goals

122
00:06:39,660 --> 00:06:41,800
rather than scoring touchdowns.

123
00:06:41,800 --> 00:06:44,940
That's why when you watch the old black and white football films,

124
00:06:44,940 --> 00:06:47,000
the field goal is actually at the front of the end zone.

125
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:49,320
That's why we call it football, right?

126
00:06:49,320 --> 00:06:51,380
It seems weird to call a game football

127
00:06:51,380 --> 00:06:54,480
that really isn't about kicking the football very often.

128
00:06:54,480 --> 00:06:56,280
So the commissioner comes out.

129
00:06:56,280 --> 00:06:58,000
He says, we've got new rules for the game.

130
00:06:58,080 --> 00:07:01,040
We've been playing the game wrong the entire time.

131
00:07:01,040 --> 00:07:02,200
This year's Super Bowl,

132
00:07:02,200 --> 00:07:05,920
we will begin keeping score by the original rules.

133
00:07:05,920 --> 00:07:08,060
And because this is America,

134
00:07:08,060 --> 00:07:09,460
and we're all submissive

135
00:07:09,460 --> 00:07:13,260
and love to unify around central concepts,

136
00:07:13,260 --> 00:07:14,360
we would all be stoked.

137
00:07:14,360 --> 00:07:14,980
No, right?

138
00:07:14,980 --> 00:07:17,380
There's gonna be an uproar.

139
00:07:17,380 --> 00:07:18,520
There's gonna be chaos.

140
00:07:18,520 --> 00:07:21,080
Outrage from fans and the teams.

141
00:07:21,080 --> 00:07:24,140
The teams have built their entire game plan

142
00:07:24,140 --> 00:07:26,560
around scoring touchdowns rather than field goals.

143
00:07:26,560 --> 00:07:27,940
Everything they've ever known,

144
00:07:28,020 --> 00:07:29,680
they've been playing this game since childhood.

145
00:07:29,680 --> 00:07:31,700
They've always been playing it

146
00:07:31,700 --> 00:07:35,380
to prioritize touchdowns over field goals.

147
00:07:35,380 --> 00:07:38,040
The fans, we don't wanna see a bunch of field goals, do we?

148
00:07:38,040 --> 00:07:39,040
No.

149
00:07:39,040 --> 00:07:40,260
We wanna see touchdowns.

150
00:07:40,260 --> 00:07:40,940
That's crazy.

151
00:07:40,940 --> 00:07:42,340
The stadium scorekeepers,

152
00:07:42,340 --> 00:07:45,420
they have decided that they are gonna keep keeping score

153
00:07:45,420 --> 00:07:47,160
by the rules that they know.

154
00:07:47,160 --> 00:07:49,640
They don't want the commissioner

155
00:07:49,640 --> 00:07:52,940
to impact the direction of the game at all.

156
00:07:52,940 --> 00:07:56,440
Most likely, the fans will continue to cheer and or boo

157
00:07:56,440 --> 00:08:00,180
based on the rules that they have watched played their entire life.

158
00:08:00,180 --> 00:08:05,440
One team in the Super Bowl decides to ignore the commissioner's announcement.

159
00:08:05,440 --> 00:08:09,120
They are gonna play the game the way that they've always played the game.

160
00:08:09,940 --> 00:08:10,940
But the other team,

161
00:08:10,940 --> 00:08:17,400
they're crazy enough to believe that the commissioner has the authority to make this decision.

162
00:08:17,400 --> 00:08:20,200
They kick a field goal and let the other team score a touchdown.

163
00:08:20,660 --> 00:08:23,280
They kick a field goal and let the other team score a touchdown.

164
00:08:23,280 --> 00:08:26,160
They kick a field goal and they let the other team score a touchdown.

165
00:08:26,160 --> 00:08:29,440
They're not even trying to stop them from scoring a touchdown.

166
00:08:29,440 --> 00:08:35,200
The scoreboard is continuing to keep score based on the old rules

167
00:08:35,200 --> 00:08:37,560
and the team that's trusting the commissioner.

168
00:08:37,560 --> 00:08:39,220
It looks like they are losing.

169
00:08:39,220 --> 00:08:42,180
It looks like everything is going wrong for them.

170
00:08:42,180 --> 00:08:43,460
But the clock hit zero

171
00:08:43,460 --> 00:08:49,180
and the confetti for the team that trusted the commissioner's word comes down.

172
00:08:49,280 --> 00:08:51,380
He comes out and hands them the trophy.

173
00:08:51,380 --> 00:08:54,840
They, in fact, are going to Disney World.

174
00:08:54,840 --> 00:08:57,040
Right?

175
00:08:57,040 --> 00:09:00,640
So, although this is a bit of a far-fetched scenario,

176
00:09:00,640 --> 00:09:06,420
especially for the size and scope of what American NFL football has come to be,

177
00:09:06,420 --> 00:09:07,920
but I think it's a helpful illustration.

178
00:09:07,920 --> 00:09:10,760
For those of us that would consider ourselves followers of Jesus,

179
00:09:10,760 --> 00:09:13,820
I think it illustrates for us the tension that we feel

180
00:09:13,820 --> 00:09:17,120
as we seek to embrace the values of the kingdom of God

181
00:09:17,120 --> 00:09:20,740
in a world that seems to be run by a totally different set of rules.

182
00:09:20,740 --> 00:09:23,940
This is how the kingdom of God works.

183
00:09:23,940 --> 00:09:27,320
The creator of the universe, the one who made the rules,

184
00:09:27,320 --> 00:09:30,680
he steps into the playing field and he says,

185
00:09:30,680 --> 00:09:32,340
we're playing by the wrong set of rules.

186
00:09:32,340 --> 00:09:34,460
We're keeping score by the wrong metrics.

187
00:09:34,460 --> 00:09:36,680
This is how life was intended to be.

188
00:09:37,360 --> 00:09:40,220
Repent and follow me.

189
00:09:40,220 --> 00:09:44,480
See, when God spoke the world into existence,

190
00:09:44,480 --> 00:09:46,860
when he breathed the breath of life into the first humans,

191
00:09:46,860 --> 00:09:47,580
it was good.

192
00:09:47,580 --> 00:09:49,300
And there was order.

193
00:09:49,300 --> 00:09:50,760
There was rules.

194
00:09:50,760 --> 00:09:51,640
There was a plan.

195
00:09:51,640 --> 00:09:52,520
But we rebelled.

196
00:09:52,520 --> 00:09:54,540
We rejected his rules and his order

197
00:09:54,540 --> 00:09:57,720
and decided to play our own game by our own set of rules.

198
00:10:00,600 --> 00:10:01,980
Honestly, when you look at the Bible,

199
00:10:01,980 --> 00:10:05,180
a quick Bible interpretation strategy here.

200
00:10:05,180 --> 00:10:09,400
You see these pages right here?

201
00:10:09,400 --> 00:10:14,220
This is the world as God intended it to be.

202
00:10:14,220 --> 00:10:16,640
And then flip all the way to the back.

203
00:10:16,640 --> 00:10:18,680
We'll go to the last like two or three pages.

204
00:10:18,680 --> 00:10:21,700
See these pages?

205
00:10:21,700 --> 00:10:23,400
Right, right.

206
00:10:23,400 --> 00:10:24,260
That's too many.

207
00:10:24,260 --> 00:10:24,660
Right here.

208
00:10:26,440 --> 00:10:28,380
That's the world as God intended it to be.

209
00:10:28,380 --> 00:10:29,940
This,

210
00:10:29,940 --> 00:10:35,260
this is the tragedy of human rebellion.

211
00:10:35,260 --> 00:10:38,220
This is God working his plan of redemption

212
00:10:38,220 --> 00:10:40,280
in the midst of our brokenness and rebellion.

213
00:10:40,280 --> 00:10:43,160
This is us playing our set of rules.

214
00:10:43,160 --> 00:10:45,420
And yet,

215
00:10:45,420 --> 00:10:48,680
those of us who have come to know and follow Jesus,

216
00:10:48,680 --> 00:10:50,540
we trust that he has the authority.

217
00:10:50,540 --> 00:10:55,140
We trust that his rules actually are the way to win.

218
00:10:56,440 --> 00:11:00,640
The rest of the story is a tragedy

219
00:11:00,640 --> 00:11:02,180
of the consequences of our rebellion

220
00:11:02,180 --> 00:11:05,400
and God's effort to restore order to his creation.

221
00:11:05,400 --> 00:11:08,540
And although this illustration will work

222
00:11:08,540 --> 00:11:10,480
for every area of our lives,

223
00:11:10,480 --> 00:11:12,620
because in every area of our lives,

224
00:11:12,620 --> 00:11:13,400
we have rebelled

225
00:11:13,400 --> 00:11:15,000
and we are keeping score

226
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:16,520
by a totally different set of rules,

227
00:11:16,520 --> 00:11:19,440
whether it comes to all of our lives,

228
00:11:19,440 --> 00:11:21,960
we are keeping score by a different set of rules.

229
00:11:21,960 --> 00:11:23,740
But I think it works particularly well

230
00:11:23,740 --> 00:11:26,800
when it comes to wealth, possessions, and finances.

231
00:11:26,800 --> 00:11:28,220
See,

232
00:11:28,220 --> 00:11:29,700
when it comes to our wealth and our possessions,

233
00:11:29,700 --> 00:11:31,680
God set the world up to be run

234
00:11:31,680 --> 00:11:33,360
by a specific set of rules.

235
00:11:33,360 --> 00:11:34,540
We can see it very clearly

236
00:11:34,540 --> 00:11:36,780
in the first page or two of Scripture.

237
00:11:36,780 --> 00:11:38,580
And we can see it very clearly again

238
00:11:38,580 --> 00:11:42,040
in the last page or two of Scripture.

239
00:11:42,040 --> 00:11:43,420
God set the world

240
00:11:43,420 --> 00:11:45,920
to be ruled by a certain set of rules.

241
00:11:45,920 --> 00:11:47,080
He placed the first humans

242
00:11:47,080 --> 00:11:48,260
in a luscious garden

243
00:11:48,260 --> 00:11:49,260
that was being hydrated

244
00:11:49,260 --> 00:11:51,900
by a river with four branches surrounding it.

245
00:11:51,900 --> 00:11:54,980
The garden was full of raw potential,

246
00:11:54,980 --> 00:11:56,060
enough resources

247
00:11:56,060 --> 00:11:57,840
for all of humanity to share.

248
00:11:57,840 --> 00:12:00,400
He invited Adam and Eve

249
00:12:00,400 --> 00:12:01,720
to be fruitful and multiply,

250
00:12:01,720 --> 00:12:03,400
to fill the earth and subdue it.

251
00:12:03,400 --> 00:12:05,580
That was enough for everyone.

252
00:12:05,580 --> 00:12:06,880
Raw potential,

253
00:12:06,880 --> 00:12:10,000
opportunity for enough things

254
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:10,700
to be produced

255
00:12:10,700 --> 00:12:12,240
for all of humanity to share,

256
00:12:12,240 --> 00:12:13,500
for all of creation

257
00:12:13,500 --> 00:12:14,560
to be taken care of.

258
00:12:17,860 --> 00:12:19,300
Harness the raw potential

259
00:12:19,300 --> 00:12:20,120
of God's creation

260
00:12:20,120 --> 00:12:20,960
for his glory

261
00:12:20,960 --> 00:12:22,040
